#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124162 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "526b792b-9952-5dae-bcd6-15564c0e013f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3131" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,\\pi)$ given that $f(x,y) = (x - y)\\cos y$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}((x - y)\\cos y) \\\\\n&= \\cos y \\\\\nf_{y}(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}((x - y)\\cos y) \\\\\n&=-\\cos y - (x - y)\\sin y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y) &= f_{x}(x, y)i + f_{y}(x, y)j \\\\\n&= \\cos yi - (\\cos y + (x - y)\\sin y)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the gradient at the specified point, we substitute\n\n$$\n(x,y)= (1,\\pi)\n$$\n\ninto the above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(1, \\pi ) &= f_{x}(1, \\pi )i + f_{y}(1, \\pi )j \\\\\n&= \\cos \\pi i - (\\cos \\pi  + (1 - \\pi )\\sin \\pi )j \\\\\n&=-i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124163 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfade25e-065a-5c66-a554-9ee4e9559a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-3132" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla g(x,y,z)$ at $(2,-1,3)$ if $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + xyz$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_{x}(x, y, z) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(x^{2} + xyz) \\\\\n&= 2x + yz \\\\\ng_{y}(x, y, z) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(x^{2} + xyz) \\\\\n&= 0 + xz \\\\\n&= xz \\\\\ng_{z}(x, y, z) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial z}(x^{2} + xyz) \\\\\n&= 0 + xy \\\\\n&= xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla g(x, y, z) &= g_{x}(x, y, z)i + g_{y}(x, y, z)j + g_{z}(x, y, z)k \\\\\n&= (2x + yz)i + (xz)j + (xy)k \\\\\n\\nabla g(2,-1, 3) &= (2(2) + (-1)(3))i + 2(3)j + 2(-1)k \\\\\n&= 1i + 6j - 2k \\\\\n&= i + 6j - 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124164 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2549d7b-07c2-5f2f-89a3-2cccecf2ba22" :question/math-academy-id "e-3133" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y-4)\\ln (x-2)$, find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The domain is $x>2$. The gradient is $$\\nabla f=\\left\\langle\\frac{y-4}{x-2},\\ln(x-2)\\right\\rangle.$$ Set both components equal to zero. Since $x-2\\ne0$, the first gives $y=4$. The second gives $x-2=e^0=1$, so $x=3$. Therefore the only point is $(3,4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124166 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67e9bcb5-4bf2-56b8-8d82-95355841eaaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3150" :question/problem "Find the gradient of $f(x,y)g(x,y)$, given $$\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=xy,&\\nabla f(x,y)&=y\\mathbf i+x\\mathbf j,\\\\g(x,y)&=\\sqrt{xy},&\\nabla g(x,y)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla [fg] = g \\, \\nabla\\! f + f \\, \\nabla\\! g\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule shown above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla [f(x, y)g(x, y)] &= g(x, y)\\nabla f(x, y) + f(x, y)\\nabla g(x, y) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{xy}(yi + xj) + xy(\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}i + \\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}j) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{xy}(yi + xj) + \\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(yi + xj) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(yi + xj)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96c73ec0-eefd-50b0-adc5-09a051ddb3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122183} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d7c01e-d6d0-54ca-99b3-3d5aed419d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122184} {:answer/id #uuid "0a14965a-37a7-5094-b356-9daa506a937e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{6}, \\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122185} {:answer/id #uuid "4042808f-9ca0-57bd-ae30-cbef5a8872fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{6}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122186} {:answer/id #uuid "76d7759c-0ceb-5096-83c5-0c4e8a743b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4042808f-9ca0-57bd-ae30-cbef5a8872fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{6}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122186} :answer-field/id #uuid "a40c9e35-d8e0-5053-81c7-0c404abc082e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da858976-fa9b-559a-a71d-31223d9b1ce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-35995" :question/problem "Given that $f(x, y) = 3x^{2} + y^{2} - x$, find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( x , y ) = \\mathbf{0} .$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=\\langle6x-1,2y\\rangle$. A vector is zero exactly when both components are zero, so $6x-1=0$ and $2y=0$. Thus the only point is $(\\tfrac16,0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5fd4a92-e5b5-52e9-9ee4-909afb389e4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24x^{3}y^{2} - 16xy^{3} + 12x^{4}y - 24x^{2}y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122243} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe3ddd6-4da3-5889-bce9-a0eeb7a0ed68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x^{3}y^{2} - xy^{3})i + (x^{4}y - 2x^{2}y^{2})j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122244} {:answer/id #uuid "22076fbb-55d8-5ee2-ae74-fe8f3c31cc86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24x^{3}y^{2} - 16xy^{3})i + (12x^{4}y - 24x^{2}y^{2})j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122245} {:answer/id #uuid "6591e636-203e-52fd-bcc0-d983663cd9e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12x^{4}y - 24x^{2}y^{2})i + (24x^{3}y^{2} - 16xy^{3})j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122246} {:answer/id #uuid "759e00c0-f41f-56ed-80b0-5ed7faf618e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16xy^{3}i + 12x^{4}yj" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22076fbb-55d8-5ee2-ae74-fe8f3c31cc86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24x^{3}y^{2} - 16xy^{3})i + (12x^{4}y - 24x^{2}y^{2})j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122245} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3599da-98d3-5c77-a0f8-929b189542d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7958e50f-73b4-5252-b8f0-e891d3332089" :question/math-academy-id "q-36144" :question/problem "Find the gradient of $g(x,y)h(x,y)$, given $$\\begin{array}{rlrlrl}g ( x , y ) & = 3 {x}^{2} - 4 y , \\, &  & \\mathrm{\\nabla } g ( x , y ) = 6 x \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - 4 \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} , &  &  \\\\ h ( x , y ) & = 2 {x}^{2} {y}^{2} , \\, &  & \\mathrm{\\nabla } h ( x , y ) = 4 x {y}^{2} \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} + 4 {x}^{2} y \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} . &  & \\end{array}$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Use $\\nabla(gh)=h\\nabla g+g\\nabla h$. The first component is $2x^2y^2(6x)+(3x^2-4y)(4xy^2)=24x^3y^2-16xy^3$. The second is $2x^2y^2(-4)+(3x^2-4y)(4x^2y)=12x^4y-24x^2y^2$. Thus $$\\nabla(gh)=(24x^3y^2-16xy^3)\\mathbf i+(12x^4y-24x^2y^2)\\mathbf j.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf1a1306-3d91-5c15-970b-52be233e0ff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{xyz}(xi + yj + zk)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122639} {:answer/id #uuid "f6749079-da6d-542b-8aa9-fe258dddaea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}i + \\frac{1}{y}j + \\frac{1}{z}k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122640} {:answer/id #uuid "fba148c8-ab55-54ac-9834-8460517b4ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xyz}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}i + \\frac{1}{y}j + \\frac{1}{z}k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122641} {:answer/id #uuid "403576a7-9ffa-509c-a9eb-ff44c3348f1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(xi + yj + zk)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122642} {:answer/id #uuid "6f491a40-70f0-591d-bdd9-d361f1561d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{xyz}(\\frac{1}{yz}i + \\frac{1}{xz}j + \\frac{1}{xz}k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6749079-da6d-542b-8aa9-fe258dddaea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}i + \\frac{1}{y}j + \\frac{1}{z}k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122640} :answer-field/id #uuid "e93cef22-d93a-5618-8d1c-dbdc7b1def35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4903eb08-1095-52c0-bd3a-a8d7c39455f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-36918" :question/problem "Given $f(x, y, z) = \\sqrt{xyz}$, find $\\nabla f(x, y, z)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Where $xyz>0$, the chain rule gives $$f_x=\\frac{yz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2x},\\quad f_y=\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2y},\\quad f_z=\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2z}.$$ Assemble these partial derivatives: $$\\nabla f=\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}2\\left(\\frac1x\\mathbf i+\\frac1y\\mathbf j+\\frac1z\\mathbf k\\right).$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acead833-458a-50b7-b6ad-7bd0acb7dd9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + 2}}i + \\frac{1}{y}j + \\frac{1}{z}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122651} {:answer/id #uuid "c1149384-c181-56ba-912d-39316ec44293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x + 2}}i + \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}j + \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122652} {:answer/id #uuid "1f84fe85-47bd-5d97-92b2-fd21b9d84818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x + 2}}i + \\frac{1}{2yz}j + \\frac{1}{2yz}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122653} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd32404-c6a6-5502-83d1-fe747f02b346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + 2}}i + \\frac{1}{yz}j + \\frac{1}{yz}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122654} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa5d355-7377-5776-b9d1-396ad4b17bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x + 2}}i + \\frac{1}{2y}j + \\frac{1}{2z}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fa5d355-7377-5776-b9d1-396ad4b17bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x + 2}}i + \\frac{1}{2y}j + \\frac{1}{2z}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122655} :answer-field/id #uuid "b55a7f93-0a4e-58d9-83ac-8a7c98e7116c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e287a6b3-141e-5735-ae69-360760a9ed55" :question/math-academy-id "q-36968" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla g(x, y, z)$ given that $g(x, y, z) = \\sqrt{x + 2} + \\ln (\\sqrt{yz})$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Use $\\ln\\sqrt{yz}=\\tfrac12\\ln(yz)$ on its domain $yz>0$. For $x>-2$, $$g_x=\\frac1{2\\sqrt{x+2}},\\qquad g_y=\\frac12\\frac{z}{yz}=\\frac1{2y},\\qquad g_z=\\frac12\\frac{y}{yz}=\\frac1{2z}.$$ Hence $\\nabla g=\\frac1{2\\sqrt{x+2}}\\mathbf i+\\frac1{2y}\\mathbf j+\\frac1{2z}\\mathbf k$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b7e2a87-ce67-5f67-a1d4-8a6c4eb8701f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 0, 0)$ and $(-1,-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122657} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a38002-2308-53c1-a529-03ccaaf1b85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 0, 0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122658} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6f8e59-1793-5329-b8a4-58ddb6e97601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1, 1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122659} {:answer/id #uuid "349fbe3f-e6f0-5177-a8dd-8b0829ff5502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1, 0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122660} {:answer/id #uuid "38d1d57d-372f-5de1-8569-70843f82de6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 0, 0)$ and $(0,-1, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38d1d57d-372f-5de1-8569-70843f82de6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 0, 0)$ and $(0,-1, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122661} :answer-field/id #uuid "e59d1134-c34c-54c5-9295-f1564b9a47aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adae0b04-7ccf-59da-b863-ae9717fcfd97" :question/math-academy-id "q-36970" :question/problem "Given $h(x, y, z) = xy + xy^{2} + z^{3}$. Find the points $(x, y, z)$ where $\\mathrm{\\nabla } h ( x , y , z ) = \\mathbf{0} .$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Set all three partial derivatives to zero: $$h_x=y+y^2=y(y+1)=0,\\quad h_y=x+2xy=x(1+2y)=0,\\quad h_z=3z^2=0.$$ The first equation gives $y=0$ or $y=-1$; either choice forces $x=0$ in the second equation. The third gives $z=0$. Therefore the points are $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-1,0)$."}]}
